= Propositional logic — Simple =

'''Propositional logic''' treats whole statements as basic units and studies how they combine.

Suppose:

<pre>
P = It is raining.
Q = The ground is wet.
</pre>

Then propositional logic can represent statements such as:

<pre>
P and Q
not P
if P then Q
</pre>

It can use truth tables to check how these combinations behave.

Propositional logic does not look inside statements to talk about individual objects or properties.

For that, [[First-order logic/Simple|first-order logic]] adds predicates and quantifiers such as “all” and “some”.

